// draft
// drafted
type Entry = { key: Text, value: Int }
// drafted
type Kwargs = { entries: Entry[unique key] }
// drafted
type CallError = { code: Text, display: Text }

// drafted
type_error: CallError = {code: "TypeError", display: "func's signature doesn't match the mapping contained in iterable"}

doublestarmap(iterable: Kwargs[]) -> Result<Int[], CallError>
	note "apply func to every item of iterable by unpacking the item's entries as keyword arguments; func and whether it accepts an item's keys are sibling obligations"
	# The output is Ok exactly when func accepts every item's keys as its keyword arguments (a
	# sibling obligation decides acceptance).
	// reaching for: `exactly_one Ok(_) in out` iff `all k in iterable:
	// func_accepts(k.entries.key)`, where `func_accepts` is a bodiless graph reference to the
	// sibling acceptance obligation. Both sides of the iff need a quantifier, but a quantifier must
	// be the last operand on a line. Folding "every item" into a predicate over the whole list
	// would move this sentence's claim into the sibling, and an in-obligation abbreviation would be
	// a second line.
	# Every Err output is the type error.
	all Err(e) in out: e == type_error
	# An Ok output has as many elements as the iterable has items.
	all Ok(res) in out: len(res) == len(iterable)
	# Each element of an Ok output is func's result on the item at the same position in the
	# iterable, so the output keeps the iterable's order.
	// reaching for: for every position i, element i of res equals func applied to item i of
	// iterable, where func is a sibling obligation called as a graph reference returning Int. That
	// is a positional zip of res with iterable mapped through func. Binders range over occurrences
	// but cannot name an index to pair the two lists, and the notation has no map of a sibling
	// function over a list to compare against res.
	// example wanted: the empty iterable: no item is unpacked, so func's signature never comes into
	// it
	// example wanted: the docstring's case, items {a: 1, b: 2} and {a: 40, b: 60} with func adding
	// a and b: each result comes from its own item, in order
	// example wanted: one item with an extra key c beside a and b, for func taking a and b: a key
	// func does not name is a mismatch
	// example wanted: one item with only key a, for func taking a and b: a missing keyword is a
	// mismatch
	// example wanted: a matching item followed by a mismatching one: the Python generator yields
	// the first result before raising, and this obligation reports only the error
	// example wanted: an item with no entries, for func taking no parameters: an empty mapping
	// matches an empty signature
